1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,363
Diya has three shirts and
four pants in her cupboard.

2
00:00:03,763 --> 00:00:06,912
Every morning she picks one
shirt and one pair of pants.

3
00:00:07,362 --> 00:00:10,521
Today she wonders how many different
outfits she can really make.

4
00:00:10,871 --> 00:00:11,771
Seven?

5
00:00:11,379 --> 00:00:12,279
Twelve?

6
00:00:11,929 --> 00:00:14,894
Is there a way to know,
without trying them all on?

7
00:00:15,394 --> 00:00:16,294
Yes.

8
00:00:15,859 --> 00:00:20,222
It is the first rule of counting, and
every counting problem in this chapter

9
00:00:20,222 --> 00:00:21,352
is built on it.

10
00:00:22,002 --> 00:00:23,865
First, let us just try them.

11
00:00:23,865 --> 00:00:26,134
Call the shirts red, blue and green.

12
00:00:26,534 --> 00:00:27,752
Pick the red shirt.

13
00:00:27,752 --> 00:00:29,985
It can go with any of the four pants.

14
00:00:29,985 --> 00:00:30,885
Four outfits.

15
00:00:31,248 --> 00:00:33,121
The blue shirt also gets four.

16
00:00:33,121 --> 00:00:34,586
So does the green one.

17
00:00:34,986 --> 00:00:36,601
Four, plus four, plus four.

18
00:00:36,601 --> 00:00:37,501
Twelve outfits.

19
00:00:37,462 --> 00:00:38,846
That is three times four.

20
00:00:39,496 --> 00:00:41,666
Here is a picture that shows why.

21
00:00:41,666 --> 00:00:43,545
It is called a tree diagram.

22
00:00:43,945 --> 00:00:47,947
The first choice splits into three
branches, one for each shirt.

23
00:00:48,347 --> 00:00:52,378
Each branch splits again into
four, one for each pair of pants.

24
00:00:52,778 --> 00:00:54,483
Count the tips at the end.

25
00:00:54,483 --> 00:00:55,383
Twelve.

26
00:00:55,072 --> 00:00:56,635
Every tip is one outfit.

27
00:00:57,235 --> 00:00:58,904
Now add a third choice.

28
00:00:58,904 --> 00:01:00,318
Two pairs of shoes.

29
00:01:00,318 --> 00:01:02,135
Every tip splits into two.

30
00:01:02,535 --> 00:01:04,122
Twelve becomes twenty four.

31
00:01:04,122 --> 00:01:05,762
Three times four times two.

32
00:01:06,412 --> 00:01:09,188
This is the fundamental
principle of counting.

33
00:01:09,188 --> 00:01:10,823
NCERT says it like this.

34
00:01:11,223 --> 00:01:15,935
If one event can happen in m different
ways, and after it another event

35
00:01:15,935 --> 00:01:20,917
can happen in n different ways, then
the two together can happen in m times

36
00:01:20,917 --> 00:01:21,817
n ways.

37
00:01:22,414 --> 00:01:23,905
And it keeps going.

38
00:01:23,905 --> 00:01:25,882
A third event with p ways?

39
00:01:25,882 --> 00:01:27,041
Multiply again.

40
00:01:27,041 --> 00:01:28,542
m times n times p.

41
00:01:29,292 --> 00:01:31,064
Why multiply, and not add?

42
00:01:31,064 --> 00:01:35,463
Because every choice of the first pairs
up with every choice of the second.

43
00:01:36,113 --> 00:01:37,109
Side by side.

44
00:01:37,109 --> 00:01:39,018
A shirt and a pair of pants.

45
00:01:39,018 --> 00:01:40,485
You make both choices.

46
00:01:40,485 --> 00:01:41,385
So multiply.

47
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:43,132
Three times four is twelve.

48
00:01:43,532 --> 00:01:44,641
Tea or coffee.

49
00:01:44,641 --> 00:01:46,514
You pick only one of them.

50
00:01:46,514 --> 00:01:47,414
So add.

51
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:51,409
Three kinds of tea, or two kinds
of coffee, gives five drinks.

52
00:01:52,009 --> 00:01:52,909
Your turn.

53
00:01:52,858 --> 00:01:55,670
A canteen has four
snacks and three drinks.

54
00:01:55,670 --> 00:01:57,885
You buy one snack and one drink.

55
00:01:57,885 --> 00:01:59,633
Seven combos, or twelve?

56
00:01:59,633 --> 00:02:00,882
Pause and decide.

57
00:02:04,082 --> 00:02:04,982
Twelve!

58
00:02:04,693 --> 00:02:07,173
You choose a snack and a drink, both.

59
00:02:07,173 --> 00:02:08,073
So multiply.

60
00:02:08,071 --> 00:02:09,255
Four times three.

61
00:02:09,905 --> 00:02:10,930
Remember this.

62
00:02:10,930 --> 00:02:14,759
When one choice follows another,
multiply the number of ways.

63
00:02:15,459 --> 00:02:17,631
Now, a worked example from the NCERT book.

64
00:02:17,981 --> 00:02:22,505
How many four letter words, with or
without meaning, can be made from the

65
00:02:22,505 --> 00:02:25,302
letters of ROSE, if no letter is repeated?

66
00:02:25,802 --> 00:02:26,702
Step one.

67
00:02:26,569 --> 00:02:27,916
Four boxes to fill.

68
00:02:27,916 --> 00:02:30,908
The first box can take
any of the four letters.

69
00:02:31,658 --> 00:02:32,558
Step two.

70
00:02:32,447 --> 00:02:36,312
One letter is used up, so the
second box has three choices.

71
00:02:37,062 --> 00:02:37,962
Step three.

72
00:02:37,935 --> 00:02:41,903
The third box has two choices,
and the last box has just one.

73
00:02:42,653 --> 00:02:43,553
Step four.

74
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:44,440
Multiply.

75
00:02:44,314 --> 00:02:46,882
Four times three times two times one.

76
00:02:46,882 --> 00:02:48,238
Twenty four words.

77
00:02:48,888 --> 00:02:50,641
What if letters may repeat?

78
00:02:50,641 --> 00:02:53,298
Then every box gets
all four letters again.

79
00:02:53,698 --> 00:02:56,006
Four times four times four times four.

80
00:02:56,006 --> 00:02:57,667
Two hundred and fifty six.

81
00:02:57,667 --> 00:03:00,801
When repeats are allowed,
the choices do not shrink.

82
00:03:01,401 --> 00:03:02,301
Quick one.

83
00:03:02,281 --> 00:03:04,455
Four flags of different colours.

84
00:03:04,455 --> 00:03:07,631
A signal uses two flags,
one below the other.

85
00:03:07,631 --> 00:03:09,592
Sixteen signals, or twelve?

86
00:03:09,592 --> 00:03:10,836
Pause and think.

87
00:03:14,036 --> 00:03:14,936
Twelve!

88
00:03:14,688 --> 00:03:19,421
A flag cannot be used twice, so the
second place has only three choices.

89
00:03:19,421 --> 00:03:20,686
Four times three.

90
00:03:21,336 --> 00:03:22,797
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:03:23,147 --> 00:03:27,173
How many three digit numbers can be
made from zero, one, two and three,

92
00:03:27,173 --> 00:03:28,218
with no repeats?

93
00:03:28,218 --> 00:03:30,834
Many students write four
times three times two.

94
00:03:30,834 --> 00:03:31,734
Twenty four.

95
00:03:32,058 --> 00:03:34,501
But a number cannot start with zero.

96
00:03:34,501 --> 00:03:37,301
So the first place has only three choices.

97
00:03:37,301 --> 00:03:38,924
Then three, then two.

98
00:03:38,924 --> 00:03:39,824
Eighteen.

99
00:03:40,325 --> 00:03:41,821
Where is this asked?

100
00:03:41,821 --> 00:03:46,480
We found no past paper question on
just this rule, so no chip lights.

101
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:51,339
The type: count numbers, words or
codes with a rule for each place, often

102
00:03:51,339 --> 00:03:53,518
with a zero or a repeat twist.

103
00:03:54,118 --> 00:03:55,253
Exam level now.

104
00:03:55,253 --> 00:03:58,225
How many four digit even
numbers can you make?

105
00:03:58,225 --> 00:03:59,408
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:04,808 --> 00:04:06,194
The answer is B.

107
00:04:06,194 --> 00:04:07,956
One hundred and eighty.

108
00:04:08,456 --> 00:04:10,340
Fill the fussy place first.

109
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:14,745
The number must be even, so the
last digit is two, four or six.

110
00:04:14,745 --> 00:04:15,657
Three ways.

111
00:04:16,157 --> 00:04:18,798
That leaves five digits
for the first place.

112
00:04:18,798 --> 00:04:21,068
Then four for the next, then three.

113
00:04:21,568 --> 00:04:22,468
Multiply.

114
00:04:22,311 --> 00:04:24,918
Three times five times four times three.

115
00:04:24,918 --> 00:04:26,520
One hundred and eighty.

116
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:28,660
Here is your recap.

117
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:30,243
Screenshot this card.

118
00:04:33,443 --> 00:04:35,629
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:04:36,029 --> 00:04:36,929
One.

120
00:04:36,474 --> 00:04:38,813
When do we multiply the number of ways?

121
00:04:39,713 --> 00:04:40,613
Two.

122
00:04:40,105 --> 00:04:42,558
How many outfits from
five shirts and two pants?

123
00:04:43,458 --> 00:04:44,358
Three.

124
00:04:43,953 --> 00:04:47,260
Why does a zero cause trouble
in the first place of a number?

125
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:51,275
Next time: five friends
line up for a photo.

126
00:04:51,275 --> 00:04:54,284
Five times four times
three times two times one.

127
00:04:54,284 --> 00:04:56,529
There is a short way to write it.

128
00:04:56,529 --> 00:04:58,265
We meet factorial notation.
